# Copy-paste template for the environment variables. Every one of them is
# documented — default, purpose, what happens with a malformed value — in
# docs/configuration.md → Environment variables, which is the reference: this file
# only shows the names: each commented value below is an example, and the table
# gives the default of every variable. Nothing here is required to run the app.
#
# The interface and the API must be served from the SAME origin. The server sends
# no Access-Control-Allow-Origin (by design), so a page loaded from another
# origin cannot read the responses — and its state-changing requests (Save, sync)
# would be rejected with 403 by the same-origin guard.
#
# In development you do not point the SPA at another backend: `bun run dev` serves
# it on :1420 and vite.config.ts proxies /health, /records, /analytics, /metadata,
# /settings, /sync and /export to the local server, so the SPA keeps using
# relative URLs. Override the proxy target with VITE_API_PROXY if your server is
# not on the default port.
#
# VITE_API_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:8799
#
# The proxy rewrites the `Host` header, so the server's host guard sees its own
# address, but it cannot hide the `Origin` header of a mutating request: start the
# server with ICE_DEV_ORIGIN set to the dev server's origin, otherwise Save / Test
# connection / Remove / sync answer 403 ("cross-origin request rejected"). The
# value must be a loopback origin.
#
# ICE_DEV_ORIGIN=http://localhost:1420
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL only makes sense for a client that is *not* a browser page:
# a standalone shell or WebView that enforces no CORS and talks to the API on its
# own. From a browser page it breaks the app, because the API cannot be read and
# cannot be written cross-origin by design.
#
# The default is no absolute base at all — relative URLs, the same origin that
# served the page — so no value is proposed here: if you need one, it is the
# origin the app server actually listens on (8731 unless changed), not the dev
# proxy port above. See docs/configuration.md → Environment variables.
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# Credentials for headless runs (scripts, CI). They count ONLY as a pair: with
# just one of the two set, both are ignored, the stored pair is used and the log
# says the env pair is incomplete. Every child process inherits them — the
# browser window the app opens included — and any process with the same user
# rights can read them.
#
# TERNA_CLIENT_ID=
# TERNA_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Seconds between two Terna calls; the accepted range is 0-10 (the default 1.2
# keeps the platform's ~1/second limit, a larger value is reduced to 10 and the
# log says so).
#
# TERNA_MIN_REQUEST_INTERVAL=1.2
#
# Escape hatch for the cache guard that refuses to replace a year with a much
# smaller snapshot: `1` disables the guard, with a line in the log.
#
# TERNA_ALLOW_YEAR_SHRINK=1

# The port the server tries first (default 8731). `--port` on the command line
# wins when both are given. A value that is not an integer between 1 and 65535 is
# ignored — the default is used (a free port if it is busy) and backend.log gets
# the line "invalid ICE_PORT value: using port N", the port actually bound —
# because a malformed variable must not decide where the app listens. An installed
# app is bound to the origin it was installed from, port included, so leave this
# alone unless you know why.
#
# ICE_PORT=8731

# Where the built interface lives. The server looks here first, then at static/
# next to the executable, then static/ next to server/, then dist/ (see
# docs/configuration.md). Only needed when the bundle is somewhere else.
#
# ICE_STATIC_DIR=C:/path/to/static

# Alternative data folder, the same thing `--data-dir` sets: the SQLite cache,
# settings.json, the encrypted secret and backend.log all live there. It is
# created with mode 0700 on macOS and Linux and with a restricted ACL on Windows,
# and only when it does not exist yet: an existing folder is never touched.
#
# TERNA_APP_DATA_DIR=C:/path/to/data
